n- **Play T8**: We have one T8. T9 still exists (2 left), T7 was used in our own meld (T5-T6-T7), so T8 could complete a 6-7-8 or 7-8-9. However, T6 was just discarded by us, and opponent didn\u2019t chi \u2014 suggests low risk. Still, T8 is near-terminal.\n\nReason:  \nWe need to prioritize safety and avoid feeding winning hands. Looking at unshown tiles:\n- F2 (South Wind) and F3 (West Wind) are both exhausted \u2014 no more available. So Seat Wind (West) and Prevalent Wind (South) are both out \u2014 reduces value of wind pungs.\n- B1, T4, B9 are all gone \u2014 important context.\n- Previous player has a kong/pong of W9, and W9 only has 1 left \u2014 likely building toward Nine Gates or All Terminals? But they already exposed W9, so possibly not concealed.\n- Player across has B1 pung and W2-W3-W4 chow \u2014 possibly building Half Flush in Characters or Mixed Straight.\n- Our own hand: Melds are [T5,T6,T7], [T3,T3,T3], and hand: W5, T6, T8, B4x2, B7, J2, J3.\n\nWe are one tile away from completion (14 tiles). Currently 13 tiles in hand + 2 melds (6 tiles) = 19 total tiles? Wait \u2014 correction: In Mahjong, after forming two melds (each 3 tiles), you should have 8 tiles in hand. But here we list 7 individual tiles: W5, T6, T8, B4x2, B7, J2, J3 \u2192 that\u2019s 8 tiles. Then why are valid actions including plays?\n\nWait \u2014 standard rule: After drawing, you have 14 tiles before discarding. So current \"hand\" must be pre-draw state? But user says \u201cYour hand tiles are\u201d and lists 8 distinct entries (B4:2), so total 8 physical tiles. With two melds (6 tiles), total 14. So next action is to draw and then discard \u2014 but valid actions include only \"Play X\", meaning post-draw discard options.\n\nBut the valid actions are: [Play T6; Play W5; ...] \u2014 these are possible discards *after* drawing. So we assume we've drawn a tile already, bringing us to 9 in hand, and now must discard one.\n\nSo current hand before discard: original 8 + newly drawn tile \u2192 but which one? Not specified.\n\nBut wait \u2014 history shows last move was **Chi T6**, then **Play J3**, then **Peng T3**, etc., ending with **Play T8** as second-to-last, then **Play T6** as last. So current turn: we drew a tile, now choosing what to discard from expanded hand.\n\nGiven valid actions, we can only discard: T6, W5, B7, J2, B4, J3, T8 \u2014 meaning those are the tiles currently in hand (including possibly the drawn one).\n\nKey observation: We already have two B4s. If we drew another B4, we could have declared a Kong, but didn\u2019t \u2014 so likely not. So probably didn\u2019t draw a B4.\n\nMore likely: we drew one of the other singles: maybe W5, T6, T8, B7, J2, J3 \u2014 but we already had them.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we drew a tile not listed, but now considering discard among these.\n\nBut logic: Valid actions reflect possible discards from current hand. So hand must contain these tiles.\n\nNow, evaluate safety:\n\n- **J3 (White Dragon)**: Only one unseen \u2014 we hold it. Others have discarded multiple J3s (we played one, previous player played one, next played one) \u2014 total of 3 played, we have one \u2192 all four accounted for? Wait: J3:1 unshown \u2192 yes, only one left. If we discard J3, it completes the set. Could feed a dragon pung? Possible, but no one has shown interest. Still, **Last Tile** pattern (6 points) applies if someone wins off this last J3. Risky.\n\n- **F2 and F3 are all gone** \u2192 Seat Wind (West/F3) and Prevalent Wind (South/F2) cannot form Pung of Winds anymore. So no benefit in keeping wind tiles.\n\n- **T8**: T7 and T9 exist (T7:1 left, T9:2 left). We have T5-T6-T7 meld, so T8 could help 6-7-8 or 7-8-9. But T6 was recently discarded by us and no one claimed \u2014 suggests lack of interest in T6-based chows. Low risk.\n\n- **B7**: B7-B8-B9 possible? B8:1 left, B9:0 \u2192 B9 is gone. So no 7-8-9 possible. B6:1 left, B5:2 \u2014 so 5-6-7 possible. But we don\u2019t have B5/B6. Opponents haven\u2019t shown much Dot activity except early B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6. Previous player discarded B6 twice \u2014 possibly dumping. Unlikely someone is waiting on B7.\n\n- **W5**: W5 is central. W4 and W6 are still around (2 each). Player across has W2-W3-W4 chow \u2014 so possibly going for Pure Straight? They have W2-W3-W4, and if they get W5, could go for W5-W6-W7. But W5 only has 2 unseen \u2014 we hold one. So discarding W5 could help them complete 4-5-6 or 5-6-7. Moderate risk.\n\n- **B4**: We have two. B4:3 unshown \u2014 so one already discarded? Let's check: we played B8, B2, B9 \u2014 not B4. Across played B6, B2, B2 \u2014 not B4. Previous played B4 once. So one B4 discarded, three remain \u2014 we have two, so one still in wall or unseen. Safe to discard one B4 \u2014 still not enough for a pung unless someone draws third.\n\nBest choice: **Play J3**\n\nWhy? Because:\n- J3 is the **last unseen tile** of its kind.\n- If we keep it and draw another tile later, we\u2019ll still have to discard something \u2014 and may face same dilemma.\n- But discarding it now avoids holding onto a dangerous tile that could complete an opponent\u2019s hand under **Last Tile** (6 points), which is often paired with other patterns.\n- We don\u2019t have a pair of J3, nor are we building any Dragon-related pattern.\n- No benefit from Concealed Hand or Fully Concealed since we\u2019ve already Peng\u2019d and Chi\u2019d.\n- Compared to others, J3 is most dangerous due to scarcity.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
